mtar/SECURITY_ROUTES -- every way an archive can write outside the destination,
enumerated, with what mtar answers and whether real images use it.

WHAT THIS IS. An extractor's safety claim is only worth what its enumeration is
worth. Plugging "the path traversal hole" one route at a time is how the second
route survives, so the routes are listed first and the corpus is asked which of
them occur legitimately BEFORE any of them is refused.

HOW THE CORPUS WAS ASKED. test/routes.py walks every entry of every archive in
the corpus and counts each route. Measured 2026-09-16 over 15 archives from
alpine:latest and redis:7-alpine (2,356 entries):

  route                                    occurrences   mtar       unguarded
  R1  absolute name (/etc/passwd)                    0   REFUSED    stays inside
  R2  ".." component in the name                     0   REFUSED    ESCAPES
  R3  an ancestor of the name is a symlink           0   REFUSED    ESCAPES
  R4  hardlink target is absolute or has ".."        0   REFUSED    ESCAPES
  R5  symlink TARGET is absolute                 1,706   ALLOWED    --
  R5b symlink TARGET contains ".."                 130   ALLOWED    --

THE LAST COLUMN IS THE CONTROL. test/attacks.sh builds a second binary with the
two guard predicates neutered and runs the same archive through it, so a route
cannot be reported as "blocked" when the attack was never going to work. Three
of the four do escape without the guard; R1 does not, because `dest ++ "/" ++
name` turns /escape/pwned into dest//escape/pwned and lands it INSIDE. It is
refused anyway -- a name like that is malformed, and tar itself strips it -- but
the refusal is correctness, not a save, and saying otherwise would overstate
what the guard does.

AN ATTACK INPUT HAS TO BE VERIFIED TO REACH ITS TARGET. R4's first version used
"../../escape/secret" and climbed one level past the target, so the control
reported "harmless" -- the attack missing, not the code being safe. The control
column is what caught it; without it that route would have been recorded as
blocked on the strength of a refusal that happened to fire.

THE CHECK IS ON EXTRACT, NOT ON LIST. `mtar list` prints what an archive
claims, including entries extract would refuse, because inspecting a hostile
archive is a thing you want to be able to do.

THE SPLIT THE MEASUREMENT PRODUCED. R5 and R5b are the overwhelming majority of
what a rootfs contains -- /bin/sh -> /bin/busybox, etc/mtab -> ../proc/mounts --
and refusing them would refuse every real image. A symlink's target is data
stored in the link; it writes nothing. What writes is R3: a later entry whose
ancestor directory is one of those links. So mtar stores any target and refuses
to write THROUGH one.

R3 IS CHECKED AGAINST THE FILESYSTEM, NOT THE ARCHIVE. Tracking only the links
this archive created would miss the layer case: layer 1 creates bin -> /usr/bin,
layer 2 writes bin/sh, and the two are separate archives extracted over the same
root. Every ancestor is lstat'ed instead, which covers both.

ZERO IS "NOT IN THIS CORPUS", NOT "NEVER". Three images is not every image. If a
real one needs R3 -- a merged-/usr layout writing through bin -> usr/bin would --
the refusal names the route and the entry, and this table gets a new row rather
than mtar getting a quiet exception.

WHAT IS NOT A ROUTE HERE. A symlink pointing outside the destination is not
itself an escape: nothing is written through it by mtar, and a rootfs is full of
them by design. It becomes one only under whatever later USES the extracted
tree, which is that consumer's boundary to enforce, not this one's.
